# an adb server for the USB device you give it (--service adb:device=usb:VENDOR:PRODUCT): the agent gets adb, not USB
#
# Build it once: sandbox-ai images build-image adb --builtin adb. adb and fastboot are Debian's own packages of the
# release (android-platform-tools, built by Debian from the Android Open Source Project: Apache-2.0) - nothing of
# Google's SDK downloads, no license to accept. Its build fetches those packages (the network, said when it builds).
# The FROM below names an exact release (Debian 12.12, 2025-09-06), pulled once and pinned by the digest it got.
#
# As a service (service.json: port=5037,data=/adb,memory=256m,pids=128) it runs beside the agent in the sandbox's
# pod with the device of its device= for itself: the agent's sandbox gets no USB device, only the adb server at
# localhost:5037 (the port every adb client asks), so `adb devices` there shows the phone. It runs as the container's
# root, which is your own user outside (rootless Podman), without any capability: the device's node is opened with
# your user's rights (the udev rule of Debian's android-sdk-platform-tools-common, or yours). Its key (the one the
# phone asks you to allow) is kept in /adb: a tmpfs, so the phone asks at every start - keep=yes in a kept start
# keeps it. A phone that is replugged gets another node: restart the sandbox then. Not together with
# --allow-host-port 5037 (the host's own adb server would be at the same port).
FROM docker.io/library/debian:12.12-slim
RUN apt-get update \
    && DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends adb fastboot tini \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    && adb version
COPY adb-service /usr/local/bin/adb-service
# The container's root on purpose (service.json "root" says why); it has no capability and no new privileges.
USER 0:0
EXPOSE 5037
# tini -s: also where Podman's own init is its PID 1 (--init, where the host has catatonit): a subreaper then.
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/bin/tini", "-s", "--", "/bin/sh", "/usr/local/bin/adb-service"]
